{"request": {"input_type": "document", "model": "voyage-4", "text": "In an interview with a customer success lead, the speaker explains how they write differently internally versus to customers: terse bullet-point messages to colleagues, and careful customer emails that never promise roadmap feature dates, give precise API details to technical evaluators, and quote only catalog list prices.\n\nInterviewer: Your team sits between sales, support and the product. How do you write?\n\nCustomer success lead: Two very different ways. Between colleagues, internally, we write bullet points and skip the formalities. No greetings, no sign-off, just what happened, what we need and by when. If a message to a colleague needs scrolling, it should have been a ticket.\n\nInterviewer: And to customers?\n\nCustomer success lead: To customers we are careful. The big one: we never promise dates for features on the roadmap. Product changes priorities, and a date in an email becomes a commitment the customer will quote back at us. We say \"it is on the roadmap\" or nothing.\n\nInterviewer: Does it depend on who reads the email?\n\nCustomer success lead: Yes. Technical evaluators, the people testing the integration, want precision. With them we link the exact page of the API documentation and give real field names. No hand-waving, no \"it should work\". If we are not sure, we say we are checking.\n\nInterviewer: What about prices? Do you talk about them?\n\nCustomer success lead: Rarely, but when we do, we use the catalog list price. Same rule as sales: the price in the email is the price in the catalog, word for word. If a customer asks about a discount, I hand over to their account owner."}, "vector": [0.0039344471879303455, 0.024307530373334885, 0.004203144460916519, -0.020993923768401146, 0.046728044748306274, -0.0321115106344223, -0.05404449999332428, 0.014742348343133926, -0.0023886156268417835, 0.052449412643909454, 0.03418859466910362, 0.07071343064308167, -0.005600821226835251, 0